WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.020
Defense in depth uses multiple layers so that failure of one control does not expose the

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:05.995
entire operation.

00:00:05.995 --> 00:00:09.950
The layers include governance, people, physical security, architecture, identity,

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:13.480
endpoint protection, monitoring, recovery, and incident response.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.915
Their value comes from how they work together.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:19.755
Physical security protects control rooms, cabinets, network equipment, field devices,

00:00:19.755 --> 00:00:21.280
and maintenance interfaces.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.420
Network segmentation restricts unnecessary pathways and creates points for monitoring.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.200
Access control assigns individual identities and least privilege where systems support

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:29.565
them,

00:00:29.565 --> 00:00:33.440
while managed gateways can protect devices with limited native capability.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.600
Remote access should use approved entry points, strong authentication, authorization,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.160
time limits, and monitoring.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.240
Connections should be disabled when not needed.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.860
Network and host monitoring can identify new devices, unexpected protocols,

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.535
cross-boundary activity, administrative changes, and abnormal behavior,

00:00:48.535 --> 00:00:52.240
but collection must be paired with review and response ownership.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.585
Vulnerability management combines inventory, vendor advisories, exposure,

00:00:55.585 --> 00:00:59.760
process consequence, testing, patch planning, and compensating controls.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.670
Backups should cover controller logic, device configuration, applications, servers,

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:04.950
and documentation.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.570
Restoration must be practiced, and recovery plans must account for physical process state.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.060
Incident response plans should include cybersecurity, operators, engineers,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.515
safety personnel, vendors, leadership, and communications.

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:20.470
Response actions such as isolation can protect systems but also remove visibility or

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.160
control.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.600
Teams need preplanned authority and safe options.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.327
Every control should be evaluated for operational effect.

00:01:26.327 --> 00:01:30.030
Test changes, schedule maintenance, define rollback, and monitor results.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.000
Defense in depth is not the number of security products deployed.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.445
It is the presence of independent,

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:38.155
verified protections that reduce credible risk while supporting safe and reliable

00:01:38.155 --> 00:01:38.840
operations.
